/** @page python_typesguide Type Conversions Between Qore and Python

    @tableofcontents

    @section python_qore_to_python Type Conversions From Qore To Python

    |!Source %Qore Type|!Target Python Type
    |\c binary|\c bytearray
    |\c bool|\c bool
    |\c date|\c datetime.datetime (absolute date/time values) or \c datetime.timedelta (relative date/time values)
    |\c float|\c float
    |\c number|\c int (an exact integral value outside the 64-bit signed integer range) or \c decimal.Decimal
    |\c hash|\c dict
    |\c int|\c int
    |\c list|\c list
    |\c string|\c str
    |\c code (closure or call reference)|\c callable

    A %Qore \c number outside the 64-bit signed integer range with an integral value that it holds exactly (as it holds
    the %Python \c int values that it is converted from) is a %Python \c int; all other numbers, including NaN and
    infinity, are \c decimal.Decimal values, so that both types round-trip.  A number converted from a \c float holds a
    binary approximation, and its \c decimal.Decimal has the shortest decimal digits that give the same value.

    A value of any other %Qore type (ex: a reference) raises a \c PYTHON-VALUE-ERROR exception.

    An absolute %Qore date/time value in the current time zone is a naive \c datetime.datetime (%Python's local time);
    a value in another time zone is an aware \c datetime.datetime with the value's UTC offset, so that the instant is
    preserved.  A relative date/time value is a \c datetime.timedelta; years are converted to 365 days and months to
    30 days.

    @note \c datetime.datetime supports years 1 - 9999 only; converting an absolute %Qore date/time value with a
    year outside this range raises an exception, as the value has no %Python representation.  When a value that cannot
    be converted is a %Qore constant being imported into %Python (a class or namespace constant), the constant is
    skipped and the rest of the class or namespace is imported normally.

    @section python_python_to_qore Type Conversions From Python to Qore

    |!Source Python Type|!Target %Qore Type
    |\c bool|\c bool
    |\c int|\c int, or \c number outside the 64-bit signed integer range
    |\c float|\c float
    |\c decimal.Decimal|\c number
    |\c bytes|\c binary
    |\c bytearray|\c binary
    |\c datetime.datetime|\c date (absolute date/time values)
    |\c datetime.date|\c date (absolute date/time values at midnight in the current time zone)
    |\c datetime.time|\c date (absolute date/time values on 1970-01-01)
    |\c datetime.timedelta|\c date (relative date/time values)
    |\c dict|\c hash
    |\c list|\c list
    |\c str|\c string
    |\c tuple|\c list
    |\c function, \c method|\c code

    All other %Python types are converted to a %Qore object using a dynamically-created class that wraps the %Python
    type or class.

    A naive \c datetime.datetime is in the current time zone; an aware one is in a time zone with its UTC offset (UTC
    for an offset of 0), and an offset with microseconds, which a %Qore time zone cannot have, raises a
    \c PYTHON-DATETIME-ERROR exception.  A \c datetime.time is a time of day on 1970-01-01, as with the time values of
    %Qore's database drivers, in the time zone of its UTC offset or in the current time zone; it is converted back to
    %Python as a \c datetime.datetime.  A \c datetime.timedelta is a relative date/time value whose components all
    have the sign of the value, so that it compares equal to the same value made from other units.

    Values that a %Qore value cannot represent raise an exception:
    - a container (\c list, \c tuple or \c dict) that contains itself, directly or through other containers, raises
      a \c PYTHON-VALUE-ERROR exception; a container referenced more than once without a cycle is converted each time
    - a string (or a \c dict key) with lone surrogates, which cannot be encoded in UTF-8, raises the %Python
      \c UnicodeEncodeError exception (\c builtins.UnicodeEncodeError)
    - an \c int with more significant bits than the maximum precision of a %Qore \c number (8192 bits, about 2466
      decimal digits) raises a \c PYTHON-VALUE-ERROR exception, as it cannot be converted exactly
*/
